  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe core idea of this strategy is to combine multi-scale optimization with physics-inspired adaptation to enhance performance. Unlike traditional optimization methods that may get trapped in local optima, this approach uses adaptive momentum and energy-dependent search ranges inspired by quantum annealing. The momentum parameter and step size dynamically adjust based on gradient magnitude, preserving feasible configurations while maintaining global search capability. The algorithm prioritizes shifts with star-shaped contribution patterns and projects gradients to preserve constraints. By minimizing the correlation between the function and its complement across all lags, we derive a bound for the Erd\u0151s constant.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over shifts k preserving support\n- Grid representation: h(x) approximated by samples over [0,2] with dx=2/n_points\n- Constraint: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\u2200i, \u222bh(x)dx=1 (implies \u2211h[i]*dx=1)\n- Surrogate for efficiency: F(h)=max(np.correlate(h,1.0-h,mode=\"full\"),\"full\") * (2.0/n_points)\n- Projection: Projected gradient descent with clipping onto feasible set {h: 0\u2264h_i\u22641, \u2211h_i=n_points/2}\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Input: n_points, optional parent solution\n   - Initialization: Start with candidate adapted from parent via interpolation\n   - Physics-inspired evolution:\n     * Use gradient descent with adaptive momentum: \u03b2 = max(0.9, 0.8 + (1-legged)/(total_iters))\n     * Adjust search range by factors based on gradient norm: \u03c3_adjustment = min(2.0, max(0.5, (grad_norm_threshold/grad_max)))\n     * Shift selection: Choose shifts with star-like high-contribution regions\n   - Momentum: Accelerated convergence via past gradient averaging\n   - Cooldown phase: Linear decrease of step size after steps fail to reduce energy\n   - Feasibility: Ensure all points follow constraints via projection\n   - Objective evaluation: Compute complete correlation profile to identify worst shift\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Base grid: n_points \u2208 {128, 256, 512}\n   - Adaptation parameters: \u03b2 \u2208 [0.8, 0.9], step_power \u2208 [-0.2, 0.05]\n   - Local search: Prioritize shift centers with maximum initial correlation contribution\n   - Global balance: Mix large jumps (10% of grid) with local parabolic fits for the worst shift\n\n5. Robustness features:\n   - Log constraints: Track extreme value to detect constraint failures\n   - Fallback projection: Reapply feasible solution when violated\n   - Score tracking: Monitor energy (C5) directly through correlation modes\n   - Cooldown management: Linear step size decay to prevent premature overheating\n\n6. Execution protocol:\n   - Timer integration: Proper time tracking at implementation level\n   - Score computation: Independent agr of compute_c5 and F(h)\n   - Constraint monitoring: Regular projection to maintain solution validity\n   - Progress tracking: Update every 100 iterations to prevent stagnation\n   - energy_threshold = 0.38\n   - Feasibility check: |max(h)|\u22641, min(h)\u22650, \u2211h=\u2248 n_points/2",
